<LibraryLayout activeSection="editorial" title="Editorial Policy & Content Standards — Ridwanah" description="Read how Ridwanah selects, writes, sources, reviews and updates health education content.">
  <div id="top" class="policy-page">
    <div class="library-breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Editorial policy</span></div>
    <section class="policy-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> TRANSPARENCY & ACCOUNTABILITY</div>
      <h1>How we handle<br /><em>health information.</em></h1>
      <p>Our editorial standards explain how content is selected, written, sourced, reviewed and corrected. Trust depends on being clear about what we know and what has not yet been checked.</p>
    </section>
    <div class="policy-content">
      <section><h2>1. Source selection</h2><p>We prioritise primary and authoritative public-health sources, including the World Health Organization, Nigeria’s Federal Ministry of Health and Social Welfare, the Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention, Africa CDC and relevant national health authorities. Research and other sources may be used where appropriate and clearly identified.</p></section>
      <section><h2>2. Writing and attribution</h2><ul><li>Use plain language while preserving the meaning and limits of the source.</li><li>Attribute announcements, estimates, targets and opinions to the organisation or people who made them.</li><li>Distinguish the date an event happened from the date a source was published.</li><li>Link directly to source material and avoid reproducing substantial copyrighted content.</li><li>Do not present a single study or news announcement as settled medical consensus.</li></ul></section>
      <section><h2>3. Medical review</h2><p>Health education content may require review by an appropriately qualified healthcare professional before being labelled medically reviewed. Ridwanah will not invent reviewer names, qualifications, registration numbers, endorsements or review dates. A review label will only be used after the reviewer and approval details have been verified and recorded.</p><p>Until then, content is labelled as an educational draft or medical review pending. Readers should use the original references and seek individual professional advice for their own health needs.</p></section>
      <section><h2>4. News and fast-changing information</h2><p>News summaries are editorial summaries of the linked publisher’s report, not independent investigations or live surveillance. Outbreak figures, advisories, policy announcements and campaign details can change. The publication date is displayed, and time-sensitive information should be checked against the current original source before being acted on or shared.</p></section>
      <section><h2>5. Updates and corrections</h2><p>Content should be revisited when authoritative guidance changes, a source link stops working or a factual error is identified. Material corrections should be reflected in the relevant page. Where practical, the page should show an updated date and a brief explanation of meaningful changes. Outdated content may be withdrawn rather than left to mislead readers.</p></section>
      <section><h2>6. Conflicts and commercial influence</h2><p>Sponsored, promotional or commercially influenced content must be clearly identified before publication. Ridwanah should not allow advertisers, product sellers or sponsors to determine medical claims or editorial conclusions. No endorsement should be implied without a documented and verified basis.</p></section>
      <section><h2>7. AI-assisted content</h2><p>AI tools may assist with research organisation, drafting or editing, but they are not a substitute for source verification or qualified clinical review. Content must be checked against the original references, and AI must never be represented as a clinician or a verified reviewer.</p></section>
      <section><h2>8. Reader safety</h2><p>Ridwanah does not diagnose, prescribe treatment, triage emergencies or provide personalised medical advice. We avoid instructions that could encourage unsafe self-treatment. The relevant disclaimer remains available throughout the website.</p></section>
      <div class="policy-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/disclaimer">Read the health disclaimer <span>↗</span></a><a class="policy-text-link" href="/privacy">Read the privacy notice <span>→</span></a></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</LibraryLayout>
